>                       Does anyone know how to force a trivial message to go
> to this daemon to test the log file?

Instead of touching a new binary.errlog, do a kill -HUP on the binlogd
process ID. Then it'll create a new file itself.
To log a entry in the file do a :

logger -b "This is a test"

This will create a entry in the binary.errlog. If you do a uerf or dia
afterwords you should be able to see it.

> 
> 4) bonus question: I need to get "yesterday's" date for ksh scripts.... any
> ideas?

date --date '1 day ago'

or

date --date '-24 hours'

or

date --date '-1 day'


I don't remember using it on Digital Unix, but it works on Linux.


